depressive and physical symptomatology. In the case of depressive symptoms, the data fit a "buffering" hypothesis       
pattern, i.e., they suggest that both social support and positive events protect one from the pathogenic effects of high
levels of life stress but are relatively unimportant for those with low levels of stress. In the case of physical       
symptoms, the data only partially support the buffering hypothesis. Particularly, the data suggest that both social     
support and positive events protect one from the pathogenic effects of high levels of stress but harm those (i.e., are  
associated with increased symptomatology) with low levels of stress. Further analyses suggest that self‐esteem and      
appraisal support were primarily responsible for the reported interactions between negative life stress and social      
